---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/loyalty';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/loyalty');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/loyalty');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Loyalty campaigns</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Shopify SMS and email: loyalty campaigns</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Use the points ledger as the source of truth. This guide sets out selection criteria and proposed workflows; it does not report hands-on platform tests or merchant campaign results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-8">
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Start with the program, then choose the channel</h2>
      <p>A loyalty reminder should explain an earned benefit: the current balance, an attainable reward, or a genuine tier deadline. Keep the loyalty system responsible for balances and eligibility. Email can explain redemption steps; reserve SMS for a time-sensitive benefit when that customer has permission to receive marketing texts. Membership alone is not SMS permission.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Supplier fit: Marsello for Shopify and POS</h2>
      <p>Marsello describes a Shopify integration connecting ecommerce and supported POS sites with a points-based loyalty program. That makes it a candidate for a retailer whose members buy in both places. This is a documented integration scope, not an independently measured retention result. Before choosing it, demonstrate how a refunded POS purchase changes the online balance and how long the update takes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Build a member event contract</h2>
      <p>Record member ID, Shopify customer ID, balance, reward threshold, tier, expiry timestamp and the last ledger update. Choose which system owns each field. A tier-change event should contain the new tier and an event ID so retries do not send duplicate celebrations. Recheck the balance before sending a redemption reminder; an enrollment-time snapshot can already be wrong.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Three proposed merchant workflows</h2>
      <p>Coffee: combine an earned reward reminder with an appropriate reorder window rather than a second discount broadcast. Beauty: warn about a real expiry date and link to eligible rewards without requiring a review. Home retailer: celebrate a tier upgrade only after online and in-store orders reconcile. These are workflow designs to validate, not campaigns we ran or quantified outcomes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Enrollment and expiration</h2>
      <p>Explain earn rules, redemption restrictions and exclusions in the invitation. A second-order invitation is one possible experiment, not a universal eligibility rule; do not silently exclude eligible discount buyers from a published program. For an actual expiry date, consider reminders thirty and seven days beforehand, checking redemption and cancellation before each send. Never invent an expiry to create urgency.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Tier changes, liability and referrals</h2>
      <p>Notify members about a real approaching tier boundary and explain the published route to retain status. Have finance assess outstanding points and redemption economics under the applicable accounting policy; points are not universally equivalent to deferred revenue. Approve stacking rules and inventory capacity before double-points promotions. Keep referral terms explicit and avoid conditioning referral access on a positive public review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Avoid promotional collisions</h2>
      <p>Give tier celebrations priority over an ordinary sale message where the offer would undermine the perk. Set a store-specific cross-channel contact cap and record which campaign was suppressed. If a member has no SMS consent, route eligible communication to another permitted channel rather than creating consent from membership. An in-store enrollment script should explain channel choices separately.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance exercise</h2>
      <p>Use test profiles with no points, a redeemable balance, a recently redeemed reward, a returned order and a changed tier. Verify merge fields, reward links, duplicate-event handling and expiry time zone. Check a member who unsubscribes between enrollment and send. These exercises have not been performed on a live merchant account.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Measure redemption without inventing lift</h2>
      <p>Track eligible members, delivered reminders, valid redemptions, reward cost and repeat orders after returns. Members who self-select into loyalty may already buy more: comparing members with non-members does not establish program lift. Where appropriate, propose a randomized reminder holdout among eligible members without withholding earned benefits. Review complaints, liability and promotion overlap each quarter.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Primary supplier reference</h2>
      <p>Checked 25 November 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.marsello.com/shopify">Marsello Shopify integration</a>. The supplier fact above is limited to that documented capability. The workflow rules and acceptance exercises are editorial proposals for a merchant to validate.</p>
    </div>
    <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm font-bold" aria-label="Related guides">
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">Vip segments →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/apps">App directory →</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
